# MSHybrid OLED backlight patch for xe (Panther Lake iGPU), board L55xJNP.
#
# STATUS as of 2026-10-26: CONFIRMED NOT NEEDED. Tested directly: backed
# up the running patched module, swapped a fully *stock* (unpatched)
# xe.ko back in (checksum-verified against the original stock backup,
# both as installed and as embedded in the regenerated initramfs), with
# only xe-dpcd-backlight.conf's enable_dpcd_backlight=3 in place -- no
# patch at all. Rebooted; user confirmed live: "the brightness controls
# work perfectly on boot as well as resuming from suspend." So the real
# fix for this panel's full backlight story (working on cold boot AND
# surviving a real suspend/resume) is entirely xe's own built-in
# enable_dpcd_backlight=3 module parameter, which forces Intel's own
# proprietary DPCD backlight interface instead of the VESA interface
# every single piece of this patch touches -- see xe-dpcd-backlight.conf
# for the full story, found live by the user, not through more kernel
# patching. The VESA-interface code path this patch modifies works fine
# from a cold boot but does not reliably survive a real suspend/resume
# on this exact panel (confirmed live: every DPCD register checkable
# from the host side reads back correctly written post-resume, matching
# the user's slider in real time, panel still visibly unresponsive --
# pointing at a TCON-firmware-level limitation in VESA mode specifically,
# not anything fixable via more patching). Kept in this repo, and
# rebuild-and-install.sh still builds/installs it on request, as
# reference and a fallback (e.g. if a future kernel/firmware update ever
# breaks the simple parameter fix) -- not because it's still required for
# this board. The currently-running kernel uses a fully stock xe.ko; this
# patch is not loaded.
#
# Five pieces:
#  1. #include "intel_pps.h" -- needed by nothing in this version of the
#     patch anymore, kept because the build is known-good with it; harmless.
#  2. intel_dp_aux_vesa_enable_backlight(): MSHYBRID_BACKLIGHT_ENABLE_PROBE
#     diagnostic logging only, no behavior change. Added 2026-10-23 to
#     find out whether this function -- the real per-enable-cycle
#     backlight callback -- is even reached on a boot where brightness
#     ends up broken, and whether drm_edp_backlight_enable() succeeds,
#     since neither of those facts were visible in dmesg before this.
#  3. intel_dp_aux_vesa_setup_backlight(): the original, minimal fix from
#     2026-09-11 -- confirmed working across many boots of kernel
#     7.0.0-34-generic -- unmodified. Firmware leaves this panel's TCON in
#     legacy PWM mode at boot, not DPCD mode; intel_dp_aux_vesa_enable_backlight()
#     (which writes BACKLIGHT_MODE_SET_REGISTER to actually switch the TCON
#     into DPCD mode) normally fixes this, but atomic state readout at boot
#     can see the encoder already enabled (inherited from the firmware-lit
#     panel) and skip calling it. Do the same AUX mode-switch + initial
#     level write right here instead, since setup (unlike enable) always
#     runs at boot regardless of that optimization.
#  4. intel_dp_aux_supports_vesa_backlight(): MSHYBRID_LUMINANCE_CAP_RELAX
#     -- the actual fix for the 2026-10-23 saga (see git log for the full,
#     warts-and-all history of everything tried and reverted before this).
#     Direct, hard evidence chain: (a) confirmed live via drm.debug that
#     xe's AUX writes to BACKLIGHT_MODE_SET_REGISTER (0x721) succeed with
#     correct values on every boot, broken or working, byte-for-byte
#     identical whether a real suspend/resume follows or not -- the panel
#     only actually responds after the real suspend/resume, proving xe's
#     normal AUX write path was never the broken piece; (b) this project's
#     own already-proven, already-shipped dGPU-mode fix
#     (clevo-control-panel's oled_luminance.py) documents, for this exact
#     panel: "DPCD 0x721 bit 7 (the luminance control enable flag) reads 0
#     by default. Setting that single bit... immediately unlocked real
#     full-range brightness on this exact panel, live-verified" -- using
#     NVIDIA's RM API directly, bypassing xe entirely, in dGPU mode; (c)
#     upstream drm_edp_backlight_enable() already has the exact mechanism
#     to set that same bit (DP_EDP_PANEL_LUMINANCE_CONTROL_ENABLE), gated
#     behind a `bl->luminance_set` flag xe's own code decides, not a live
#     capability probe; (d) xe's own gate for that decision
#     (intel_dp_aux_supports_vesa_backlight()) requires BOTH
#     DP_EDP_PANEL_LUMINANCE_CONTROL_CAPABLE and
#     DP_EDP_SMOOTH_BRIGHTNESS_CAPABLE, while oled_luminance.py's
#     already-proven check only ever required the first one -- and xe's
#     own dmesg on this exact panel has only ever logged the *other*
#     branch's "AUX Backlight Control Supported!" message, never "AUX
#     Luminance Based Backlight Control Supported!", meaning the stricter
#     combined check fails here despite the panel demonstrably supporting
#     luminance control. Relaxed to match the simpler, already-proven
#     check -- CONFIRMED WORKING live 2026-10-23 on kernel
#     7.0.0-38-generic: dmesg now logs "AUX Luminance Based Backlight
#     Control Supported!" (never once logged before this fix, on any
#     boot, all night), max_brightness changed from the legacy 4095
#     scale to 512 (the real luminance scale), and the brightness slider
#     was confirmed working perfectly after a normal cold boot -- no
#     suspend/resume, no load-order tricks, no other workaround needed.
#  5. MSHYBRID_LUMINANCE_REASSERT (added 2026-10-26, intel_dp_aux_backlight.c
#     + .h + intel_display_debugfs.c): the capability-relax fix above got
#     brightness working on cold boot, but confirmed live that it stops
#     working again after a suspend/resume -- the DPCD 0x721 luminance-
#     enable bit gets cleared by something after xe's own enable_backlight()
#     path already correctly re-wrote it (confirmed via drm.debug: the
#     right value lands at 0x721 multiple times during a real resume, panel
#     still ends up unresponsive). This exact bug was already found and
#     fixed once, on the dGPU-mode NVIDIA-RM-API path for this identical
#     panel/register (clevo-control-panel's oled_luminance_daemon.py):
#     "resets as a side effect of *any* Mutter display mode-set," not
#     confined to one catchable trigger, fixed there with a periodic,
#     idempotent reassert rather than trying to enumerate every cause.
#     xe has no public interface for a cheap, targeted single-register DPCD
#     write the way NVIDIA's RM API gives userspace (confirmed: no raw AUX
#     debugfs path exists here beyond i915_dp_force_link_retrain, which
#     forces a full, heavier link retrain -- confirmed live that triggering
#     it produced no dmesg activity at all when the link was already
#     considered trained, so it isn't even a reliable lever here). Adds a
#     new debugfs file, `mshybrid_luminance_reassert` (0644, same
#     per-connector debugfs directory as the existing i915_dp_* entries),
#     that does the same minimal, idempotent read-then-write
#     oled_luminance.py's own enable_luminance_mode() does: read
#     BACKLIGHT_MODE_SET_REGISTER, write it back with the luminance-enable
#     bit added only if it isn't already set. A no-op on any connector not
#     actually using luminance mode. Intended to be polled by a small
#     userspace reassert service (not yet built as of this commit) the same
#     way oled_luminance_daemon.py polls on the dGPU side.
